        <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">

<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">A three-phase system is balanced when all phase voltages are equal in magnitude and 120&deg; apart. In real plants &mdash; large single-phase loads, arc furnaces, rectifiers, uneven feeder impedances &mdash; that ideal breaks. The result is negative-sequence current that heats motors, derates transformers, and distorts the waveform. This article covers the causes, the cost, and why a low-voltage (LV) SVG + AHF is the dynamic correction.</p>
<h2 id="how-unbalance-is-quantified" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">How Unbalance Is Quantified</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid leading-relaxed mb-6">The standard metric (NEMA MG-1 / IEC 60034) expresses voltage unbalance as a percentage:</p>
<blockquote class="border-l-4 border-brand-orange pl-4 italic text-text-mid my-6">V_unbalance% = (maximum deviation from average phase voltage) / (average phase voltage) &times; 100%</blockquote>
<p class="text-text-mid leading-relaxed mb-6">Systems are typically classified as unbalanced above about <strong>1&ndash;3%</strong>. IEC 61000-3-13 outlines the acceptable limits.</p>
<h2 id="major-causes" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Major Causes</h2>
<ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mb-6 text-text-mid leading-relaxed">
  <li><strong>Uneven load distribution</strong> &mdash; unequal loading across the three phases.</li>
  <li><strong>Large single-phase loads</strong> &mdash; heaters, welders, and EV chargers on one phase draw excessive current.</li>
  <li><strong>Line asymmetry</strong> &mdash; unequal impedance or a non-symmetric line configuration, worse on long feeders.</li>
  <li><strong>Faulty equipment</strong> &mdash; damaged transformers or poor connections.</li>
  <li><strong>Distributed generation</strong> &mdash; asymmetrical PV or ESS injection can unbalance a weak bus.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="what-it-costs" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">What It Costs</h2>
<ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mb-6 text-text-mid leading-relaxed">
  <li><strong>Reduced motor efficiency</strong> &mdash; negative-sequence current produces a counter-rotating field; a 1% voltage unbalance can raise motor temperature by ~5&ndash;10%.</li>
  <li><strong>Equipment overheating</strong> &mdash; extra losses accelerate aging and raise failure risk.</li>
  <li><strong>Increased energy consumption</strong> &mdash; unbalanced voltage raises system losses and the bill.</li>
  <li><strong>Voltage fluctuations</strong> &mdash; unbalance worsens flicker on other loads.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="why-fixed-methods-fall-short" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Why Fixed Methods Fall Short</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid leading-relaxed mb-6">Load balancing (rewiring) and automatic voltage regulators (AVR) help, but they are slow or manual. A switched capacitor bank can correct average PF but cannot rebalance per phase or track a swinging load &mdash; and it risks resonance.</p>
<h2 id="how-an-lv-svg-ahf-fixes-it" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">How an LV SVG + AHF Fix It</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid leading-relaxed mb-6">A static var generator (SVG) &mdash; a voltage-source converter on high-frequency IGBTs &mdash; injects or absorbs reactive current <strong>per phase</strong>, independently, to rebalance voltages and hold PF near unity: sub-cycle (<strong>&lt; 1 ms</strong>) response, continuous <strong>&plusmn;1%</strong> regulation, PF held at <strong>0.99+</strong>. It cancels the negative-sequence component a fixed bank cannot touch.</p>
<p class="text-text-mid leading-relaxed mb-6">An active harmonic filter (AHF) removes the 5th, 7th, 11th, and 13th distortion that nonlinear loads add on top:</p>
<blockquote class="border-l-4 border-brand-orange pl-4 italic text-text-mid my-6">i_c* = i_L &minus; i_s(fundamental)</blockquote>
<p class="text-text-mid leading-relaxed mb-6">Synthesized by a bridge switching at 15&ndash;20 kHz, it tracks within <strong>&lt; 1 ms</strong> and pulls THDi from <strong>30%+ down to &lt; 5%</strong>. Together, AHF + SVG give continuous, adaptive phase balancing as the load varies &mdash; no fixed tuning, no resonance risk.</p>
<h2 id="igbt-vs-sic" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">IGBT vs SiC</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid leading-relaxed mb-6">Conventional LV AHF and SVG units use IGBTs capped near <strong>150 &deg;C</strong>, switching typically below <strong>20 kHz</strong>. Migrating to SiC MOSFETs (<strong>200 &deg;C</strong> junction, ~<strong>10&times;</strong> breakdown field, <strong>2&ndash;3&times;</strong> switching frequency, ~<strong>50%</strong> lower switching loss) widens control bandwidth and shrinks the cabinet. That is the direction CHITEK’s LV AHF + SVG platform is built on.</p>
<h2 id="engineering-deployment-checklist" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Engineering Deployment Checklist</h2>
<ol class="list-decimal text-text-mid leading-relaxed mb-6 pl-5 space-y-2">
  <li>Measure phase voltages and currents at the PCC over a full duty cycle to find the worst unbalance.</li>
  <li>Place the SVG/AHF at the unbalanced feeder, close to the offending load.</li>
  <li>Audit capacitor banks for resonance and mistuning before commissioning.</li>
  <li>Check weak-grid margin and size for the worst-case dip.</li>
  <li>Leave headroom (15&ndash;25%) for added single-phase load.</li>
</ol>
<h2 id="typical-application-profiles" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Typical Application Profiles</h2>
<ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mb-6 text-text-mid leading-relaxed">
  <li><strong>EV charging hubs</strong> &mdash; single-phase charger clusters unbalance the bus; the SVG rebalances per phase.</li>
  <li><strong>Arc-furnace and steel plants</strong> &mdash; violent, unbalanced reactive swings; AHF + SVG stabilize.</li>
  <li><strong>C&amp;I plants with mixed single/three-phase load</strong> &mdash; a combined unit cuts heating and PF penalties.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="bottom-line" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Bottom Line</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid leading-relaxed mb-6">Voltage unbalance is a negative-sequence problem that fixed capacitors cannot solve and that silently erodes motor and transformer life. A low-voltage <strong>AHF + SVG</strong> pair restores balance and waveform quality at the source, adaptively. CHITEK’s LV AHF + SVG platforms are specified from exactly this measurement-first discipline.</p>
<p class="text-text-mid leading-relaxed mb-6">What is your phase-voltage unbalance at the PCC &mdash; and which phase is carrying the extra load?</p>

